WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:09.340
In 1983 Argentina returned to a

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:13.719
democracy and at 6 years old I first

00:00:13.719 --> 00:00:16.810
used a computer. It was love at

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.570
first sight. I lived in a small rural

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.820
town with ​​2000 people in the interior of

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.640
my country and my elementary school had

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.940
bought a computer. Just one for

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.400
the entire school and the only one in the whole

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:31.910
town.

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:34.400
Every Saturday morning, between the ages of

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.190
9 and 14 years old, my parents took me 80

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:40.010
miles so that I could learn

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:42.770
programming. We didn't own a vehicle

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:45.020
and there was no public transportation,

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.170
but since my dad is a mechanic, some of

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:50.059
their clients lent us their vehicles

00:00:50.059 --> 00:00:52.970
so we could make that trip.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.449
When I was 15, I had to start working

00:00:55.449 --> 00:00:58.670
at my first job: teaching

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.460
computing. Since that day I have not stopped

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.369
teaching and I continue to learn computer science.

00:01:04.369 --> 00:01:06.950
What brings me here today to chat with

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.380
you is to tell you about two of my

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:13.430
passions: Teaching and programming. I am Yanina

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:15.470
Bellini Saibene, a researcher at the

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.119
Argentine National Institute of

00:01:17.119 --> 00:01:17.869
Agricultural Technology.

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:21.380
I am an organizer and part of the R-Ladies

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:24.350
Global Team, I am a co-founder of LatinR,

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:27.410
I am part of MiR and co-founder of

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:30.380
MetaDocencia. Today we are going to learn about

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.420
the learnr package to make interactive

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.670
tutorials and I hope you can

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.580
share the same enthusiasm that I

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:38.570
felt on those trips

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:41.810
on Saturday mornings.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:44.360
The popular wisdom of the Andean

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:49.100
highlands tells us that if I hear something, I

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:50.560
I forget it.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.880
If I see something, I remember. But if I do something,

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.899
I learn it. The learnr package makes it easy to

00:01:58.899 --> 00:02:01.479
convert an R Markdown document

00:02:01.479 --> 00:02:04.090
like this we see on the left, into a

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.549
interactive tutorial like we see

00:02:06.549 --> 00:02:09.429
on the right. We can generate tutorials,

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:11.470
statistics, data science,

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:13.599
programming or whatever you can

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:17.050
imagine. These types of tutorials have

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:19.269
content along with interactive

00:02:19.269 --> 00:02:22.150
components that allow us to verify

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.510
and reinforce understanding. As we see in

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:28.269
this example tutorial, the content

00:02:28.269 --> 00:02:30.340
can present in the form of text,

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.250
images, and diagrams. We can also

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.430
add videos, graphics and customize

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:39.880
the style of our tutorial. On the left,

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.099
we have a content and progress bar

00:02:42.099 --> 00:02:44.680
that allows us to jump

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:46.930
between the different topics and how far

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:49.810
we have gone. Learnr allows us to

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:51.970
learn by doing by transforming

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:53.890
code chunks into interactive

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:55.280
exercises.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.540
For example, this exercise shows

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:59.450
us the code to create a

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:02.390
graph, if we press the

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.020
Run Code button the result will be shown

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:09.020
below. If we change something in the

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.180
code, for example we swap

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.150
the variables of the x and y axes, and we

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:17.090
execute again, that change will be reflected

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:20.060
in the result and if we make a mistake,

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.400
for example, we forget

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.670
to indicate which variable is the x axis

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:28.269
when we execute it, a message will be shown

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:31.290
indicating that error.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.959
These types of exercises allow

00:03:33.959 --> 00:03:36.299
students to test their

00:03:36.299 --> 00:03:39.299
knowledge and reflect on what

00:03:39.299 --> 00:03:42.560
they learned. We can also generate

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:45.689
multiple-choice questions, indicating

00:03:45.689 --> 00:03:47.909
which answer or answers are

00:03:47.909 --> 00:03:50.730
correct and selecting a different

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.799
message to show our

00:03:52.799 --> 00:03:55.560
students according to the selection they

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.709
have chosen. Finally, another great

00:03:58.709 --> 00:04:00.840
advantage of this type of tutorial,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:04.049
is that they can be delivered as a

00:04:04.049 --> 00:04:06.840
R package and then once installed,

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:08.939
they will appear in the Tutorial tab

00:04:08.939 --> 00:04:11.879
within RStudio, or they can be

00:04:11.879 --> 00:04:14.430
published as a web page.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:17.190
It is very easy to share this material

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:20.090
in this way.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:23.320
The question is now: how do you generate a good

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:26.020
tutorial? I'm going to tell you my own

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:28.570
secret recipe, based on the teachings

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:31.930
in this book. We can think of this

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:33.910
type of educational material as having two

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:36.430
main components: context and

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:39.300
content, and the context will strongly

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.730
influence the final content. To

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:44.680
begin to define this context, the

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:46.570
first question I have to answer

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:49.480
is: who am I generating this

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:52.660
tutorial for? Are they newbies? Are they

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:55.330
beginners? What do they need to

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:56.950
know in order to take advantage of what

00:04:56.950 --> 00:05:00.280
I want to teach them? Do they have specific

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.410
needs such as

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:06.310
accessibility? The first step to

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:08.590
starting the design is knowing my

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:11.680
audience and Persona Types are a very

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.470
good tool for this particular

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:15.889
stage.

00:05:15.889 --> 00:05:17.960
The second question I have to

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.539
answer is: what do I want you to learn from

00:05:20.539 --> 00:05:22.909
this tutorial? Here I have to define

00:05:22.909 --> 00:05:25.700
what I am going to teach.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:28.160
I have to write down my learning goals.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.980
At this particular stage

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:33.650
It is very useful for me to generate a

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:35.690
Concept Map with all the concepts that

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:37.610
I want to teach and how they relate

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:39.289
to each other.

00:05:39.289 --> 00:05:42.979
My third step is to search and see if

00:05:42.979 --> 00:05:45.669
there are lessons on this topic.

00:05:45.669 --> 00:05:49.849
Analyzing how other people teach, what

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:52.129
type of tool they used, and the

00:05:52.129 --> 00:05:53.960
sequence they present the

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:57.139
content can save a lot of time

00:05:57.139 --> 00:06:00.409
and give us very good ideas. A bonus is

00:06:00.409 --> 00:06:03.379
if that material is shared

00:06:03.379 --> 00:06:06.860
in a way that can be reusable.

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:09.929
Finally and related to this last point,

00:06:09.929 --> 00:06:11.929
I have to answer the question

00:06:11.929 --> 00:06:14.879
What am I going to let other people do

00:06:14.879 --> 00:06:17.610
with this material? So here I have to

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:20.219
select a user license that is

00:06:20.219 --> 00:06:22.649
very clear and explicit, so that both my

00:06:22.649 --> 00:06:25.619
students and colleagues can know

00:06:25.619 --> 00:06:27.749
how to use my material and do it the

00:06:27.749 --> 00:06:30.080
right way.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:33.020
Yes, I know, there is a lot of work to do

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:34.879
before writing a single line of

00:06:34.879 --> 00:06:37.729
code. No don't worry, I have a

00:06:37.729 --> 00:06:39.409
package that will help you with this

00:06:39.409 --> 00:06:42.530
task. From MetaDocencia we have created a

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:45.469
learning package with templates in

00:06:45.469 --> 00:06:48.650
Spanish, where we suggest which

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:50.810
sections and parts a tutorial can

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:53.889
have. Let's see how it works.

00:06:53.889 --> 00:06:56.360
We need to have the learnr package

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:58.370
installed, which can be found on CRAN,

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:00.680
and the development version of the

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:02.930
learnres package found online, which

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:06.340
contains the templates in Spanish.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:09.010
Then to generate a new tutorial, I'm going to

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:11.949
select the File -&amp;gt; New File

00:07:11.949 --> 00:07:15.040
R Markdown and there I will choose From

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:17.470
Template. From the list of templates that

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.210
appear I'm going to choose Interactive Tutorial

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:22.389
sections. This new

00:07:22.389 --> 00:07:24.340
document that is created tells us

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:26.110
in the header that it will be a

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:28.570
learnr tutorial and several

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:31.870
options appear with their parameters and what

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:35.050
each of them mean.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:38.880
We see there is already preloaded content,

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.800
with sections and suggestions. We are going to

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:44.229
run this document to see that,

00:07:44.229 --> 00:07:47.680
in addition, we have customized how

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:50.680
the fonts, colors, and links

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:53.320
are presented; with the visual identity of

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:55.300
MetaDocencia. You can find out how

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:57.910
to customize this using our

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:01.240
css file that is installed along with the

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:02.470
package.

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:06.400
Now this first structure that

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:08.020
we propose that all your tutorials

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:11.290
have, presents the learning objectives

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:13.780
as the first option; with the idea that

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:15.550
a person who comes across our

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:19.210
tutorial, at first glance,

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:21.520
knows what they are supposed to

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:24.250
learn with it. Also, every time

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.620
You see the box with the little apple,

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:29.620
we are presenting an example there and

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:31.990
we also make available links to

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:34.360
places where you can learn more about

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:36.880
this tool or with examples generated by

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:38.460
others.

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:40.530
The second content that we suggest you

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:43.170
present is the Persona Type;

00:08:43.170 --> 00:08:45.030
Again, so that whoever is going to do the

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:49.260
tutorial, knows who was thought of when

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:52.020
that content was generated. We're

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.840
Putting links to several definitions of

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:57.540
Persona Types made by RStudio and

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:00.180
by MetaDocencia.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:03.210
Finally, we present the user license

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:06.240
and here we explain the difference

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:08.520
between the licenses that are for the

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:11.310
educational material: the explanation,

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:16.020
slides, videos; and the source code. And

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:18.290
we also present an example.

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:21.330
Finally, as an optional part of

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:23.970
these tools, we present a Concept Map,

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:26.430
with an example and links to

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:29.010
where to go learn more about concept maps

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:33.270
and where you can see and

00:09:33.270 --> 00:09:35.820
use several concept maps already

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:38.400
generated related to data science

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:40.550
and the teaching of R.

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:43.250
In case you are going to add this

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:46.550
section, it is recommended to put it after

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:49.760
the learning objectives. Finally,

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:52.250
the other two sections that appear in

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:54.740
this tutorial template present an

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:57.470
example of an exercise with and without code

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:01.240
and a multiple choice question.

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:04.160
Once the context is defined we have to

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.620
generate the content of the lesson. There

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:08.930
are many processes to generate lessons

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:11.450
effectively. At least we will have to

00:10:11.450 --> 00:10:14.450
answer two questions: what will be the

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:16.519
presentation sequence of that

00:10:16.519 --> 00:10:18.980
content? and how are we going to help

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:21.470
our students to check their

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:24.050
understanding of each topic? The

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:26.810
formative assessments presented at

00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:28.850
different times throughout the

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:30.829
tutorial are an excellent tool

00:10:30.829 --> 00:10:34.190
to achieve this goal of reflecting

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:36.649
on what has been learned, checking the

00:10:36.649 --> 00:10:40.130
concepts learned and allowing validation

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:42.470
of the progress that we are

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:43.920
making.

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:47.519
The interactive exercises and

00:10:47.519 --> 00:10:50.010
multiple choice questions are both

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:51.630
tools that learnr provides us to

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:54.360
to help us in that task.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:56.550
A chunk of code becomes a

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:59.670
exercise when I give it a name and

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:03.630
set the exercise parameter to `true`.

00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:05.670
From there learnr generates a code box

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:07.890
with Run Code and Start Over

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:10.920
buttons, which can have code or

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:13.500
be empty. If one writes the code

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:15.779
we will have very useful functionalities

00:11:15.779 --> 00:11:17.940
such as, for example, suggestions to

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:20.730
complete what we are writing or

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:23.339
it will also mark possible syntax

00:11:23.339 --> 00:11:27.779
errors. For these exercises, as

00:11:27.779 --> 00:11:30.029
I was saying, I can present them as

00:11:30.029 --> 00:11:33.750
blank or can I present them with code and

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:36.839
that code can be complete, it can

00:11:36.839 --> 00:11:38.820
be functional code, or it can be

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:41.459
incomplete. Which presents us with

00:11:41.459 --> 00:11:44.399
a range of possibilities to perform

00:11:44.399 --> 00:11:45.959
exercises.

00:11:45.959 --> 00:11:48.660
I can present functional code and

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:50.279
ask students to change it

00:11:50.279 --> 00:11:52.860
to do something else. I can present

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:54.930
code that has an error and ask

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:58.019
students to correct it, or I can

00:11:58.019 --> 00:12:00.030
submit code that is

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:03.470
missing parts, such as a faded

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:06.420
exercise, which students have

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:09.870
to complete correctly.

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:13.560
These interactive exercises also

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:15.300
have the possibility for one to

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:18.270
add tips or hints, and solutions.

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:21.420
To do that we generate another code chunk,

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:23.460
which will have the same name,

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:25.740
but to which we add a word:

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:28.800
"-hint". If we want more than

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:31.110
one, after the word hint we will

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:32.910
put a "-1", "-2",

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:36.080
and so on according to the number of

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:38.220
hints that we want to give to

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:40.800
students. To add a solution

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:44.220
we write another code chunk, again

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:45.960
with the same name, and in this

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:49.980
case will be "-solution". Everything

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:51.720
we put in there will be presented

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:54.930
with a button called Hints,

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:57.390
in this code box, and which we will

00:12:57.390 --> 00:12:59.790
be able to navigate. The last one

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:02.220
presented is the solution. We also have

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:04.080
the option of copying to the

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:07.500
clipboard what it says on each line and

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:09.570
pasting it into your code so you don't have to

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:11.960
type it from scratch.

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:14.270
The other element that we have to be able

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:17.360
to conduct formative assessments are the

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:19.820
multiple choice questions. For this

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:22.900
learnr gives us the quiz question

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:27.110
and answer functions. There we can define

00:13:27.110 --> 00:13:29.510
the text of the question, what the

00:13:29.510 --> 00:13:31.760
answers are, with the correct parameter

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:33.650
equal to true, we will indicate

00:13:33.650 --> 00:13:36.290
which one is correct and in

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:38.150
the message parameter we will be able to

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:40.010
write the text we want to give as

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:43.050
feedback to our students.

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:44.970
We also have the possibility to

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.670
indicate if we are going to allow the

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:50.070
student try again, in case they

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:52.290
were wrong, with the parameter

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:55.620
allow retry equal to true; and we have, in

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:57.690
the case of questions, the option

00:13:57.690 --> 00:13:59.940
to change the text of the

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:02.940
submit button and try again button,

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:04.800
allowing us to put it in

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:07.079
our language. Hopefully soon

00:14:07.079 --> 00:14:09.540
let's have that functionality also in

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:12.570
the code exercises. Multiple choice

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:15.060
answers are a great way to

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:18.690
catch common mistakes

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:21.420
students often make when

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:24.480
they learn a new topic. If we

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:27.420
we design them properly and include this

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:30.360
type of common error in the

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:34.410
wrong answer, we can detect it and the

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:36.180
feedback we give them in the

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:38.010
messages will be much more

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:41.130
meaningful to help them learn

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:43.829
the subject well and continue on their

00:14:43.829 --> 00:14:46.670
learning path.

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:49.970
These are just two of the tools and

00:14:49.970 --> 00:14:53.600
we have seen them quickly. There are

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:57.050
many other packages, which added to

00:14:57.050 --> 00:14:59.780
learnr will allow us to carry out and

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:02.480
extend the type of feedback and

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:03.860
interaction we can have with

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:06.470
our students. Let's mention

00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:08.690
some. For example, gradethis, to make

00:15:08.690 --> 00:15:11.480
sure that the written code is

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:14.600
correct. We have a glossary, which will

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:17.240
allow us to have definitions of concepts

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:19.520
that will be seen during the lesson.

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:21.740
Parsons, in order to design Parsons problems.

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:25.220
DiagrammeR to be able to make and

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:26.990
present diagrams and mind maps,

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:30.670
among many others.

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:33.230
We have reviewed the learnr package to

00:15:33.230 --> 00:15:35.690
generate interactive tutorials and what

00:15:35.690 --> 00:15:37.970
elements should not be missing in the design

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:39.920
of a tutorial. If you want to continue

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:42.680
exploring this topic, I leave the link of

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:44.899
the web page of this talk, where there are

00:15:44.899 --> 00:15:47.600
courses, blog post, tutorials and packages.

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:50.050
All in Spanish and dedicated to this topic.

00:15:50.050 --> 00:15:53.839
The MetaDocencia website, where we teach

00:15:53.839 --> 00:15:56.149
how to teach for free and in

00:15:56.149 --> 00:15:58.430
Spanish, and the link to the official

00:15:58.430 --> 00:16:00.740
package website, which is in English.

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:02.660
Now you have a lot of

00:16:02.660 --> 00:16:04.790
tools to generate your own

00:16:04.790 --> 00:16:07.279
materials and be able to share them with

00:16:07.279 --> 00:16:09.510
the entire R community.

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:13.380
Lastly, I wanted to tell you that learning

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:17.100
is to acquire power and it is a very special

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:20.370
power, because it does not wear out with use

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:23.550
And once acquired, no one can take

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:24.889
it away.

00:16:24.889 --> 00:16:28.459
That's why when we teach, when

00:16:28.459 --> 00:16:31.489
we share our material, we make it

00:16:31.489 --> 00:16:35.359
reusable, we generate it in our language,

00:16:35.359 --> 00:16:38.449
we are delivering power.

00:16:38.449 --> 00:16:40.369
I invite you to share your

00:16:40.369 --> 00:16:42.819
knowledge, to share your power.

00:16:42.819 --> 00:16:47.209
Who knows, maybe a girl or a boy, in

00:16:47.209 --> 00:16:49.339
a small rural town, with a

00:16:49.339 --> 00:16:52.489
computer in their school, finds one

00:16:52.489 --> 00:16:55.279
of our tutorials and open the

00:16:55.279 --> 00:16:58.669
door to new knowledge. To a

00:16:58.669 --> 00:17:00.980
knowledge that changes their life in a

00:17:00.980 --> 00:17:05.079
positive way ... and why not ours too.

00:17:05.079 --> 00:17:09.579
Thank you very much for listening to me.

